Object.assign(RHEMA_ENGLISH_BIBLE_DICT, {
  covenantlawsuit: 'Prophetic pattern where God brings a legal-style charge against His people for covenant unfaithfulness.',
  witnessstone: 'A stone or monument serving as a witness/reminder of an agreement or event.',
  pillar: 'Can be a physical column, memorial stone, or symbolic support depending on context.',
  memorial: 'An object, act, or day meant to keep an event or truth remembered before people and/or God.',
  vow: 'A solemn promise made to God. Biblical vows should be read carefully because they carry obligation.',
  oath: 'A solemn sworn statement or promise, often invoking God as witness.',
  lots: 'Objects used for making a decision or assigning portions. The practice appears in both OT and NT settings.',
  covenantcut: 'Ancient covenant-making could involve cutting animals, symbolizing the seriousness of the agreement.',
  saltcovenant: 'Phrase for a lasting covenant; salt can symbolize permanence, preservation, or loyalty in ancient settings.',
  bloodcovenant: 'A covenant ratified with blood or sacrifice. Context determines the exact ritual and meaning.',
  suzerain: 'A great king or overlord in ancient treaty language. Helpful background for some covenant discussions.',
  vassal: 'A lesser king/people bound to a greater king by treaty obligations.',
  tribute: 'Payment given to a ruler or foreign power, often as taxation, submission, or political obligation.',
  census: 'A counting of people, often for military, tax, or administrative purposes. In some biblical contexts it raises trust/obedience issues.',
  ransom: 'A price paid for release. In biblical usage it can be literal, legal, or theological depending on context.',
  pledge: 'Something given as security or guarantee. OT law regulates pledges to protect vulnerable people.',
  surety: 'A guarantee for another person\'s debt or obligation. Proverbs often warns about the risk of becoming surety.',
  usury: 'Older English term for interest on a loan. OT law especially restricts exploiting the poor through interest.',
  glean: 'To gather leftover grain or produce after harvest. OT law protected the poor, widow, orphan, and sojourner through gleaning.',
  gleaning: 'Gathering leftover harvest. In Ruth, gleaning provides the setting for provision and kindness.',
  widow: 'A woman whose husband has died. Scripture often highlights widows as vulnerable people God commands His people to protect.',
  orphan: 'A child without parental protection. Often paired with widow and sojourner as people needing justice and care.',
  fatherless: 'Often means an orphan or child without a protecting father; a frequent concern in OT justice texts.',
  poor: 'Can refer to economic need, vulnerability, humility, or dependence. Context decides the focus.',
  needy: 'Those lacking resources or protection; often paired with poor in justice and mercy contexts.',
  oppressed: 'People burdened or mistreated by power, injustice, slavery, or violence.',
  hireling: 'A hired worker. In some contexts it contrasts with someone who has true care or ownership.',
  bondservant: 'A servant/slave under obligation. English translations vary; ancient slavery/servitude does not map neatly onto one modern category.',
  slave: 'A person owned or bound in service. Biblical contexts include ancient social realities and metaphorical uses; read carefully.',
  servant: 'Can mean household servant, official, slave, or devoted representative depending on context.',
  handmaid: 'Older English for female servant or maidservant.',
  maidservant: 'A female servant. Context decides social status and role.',
  concubines: 'Secondary wives or lower-status marriage partners in ancient settings.',
  barren: 'Unable to bear children. In biblical narrative it often appears in stories of promise, shame, waiting, and divine provision.',
  womb: 'Can refer literally to pregnancy/birth or figuratively to origin, compassion, or hidden formation.',
  seed: 'Can mean plant seed, offspring/descendants, or promised line. Context is crucial.',
  offspring: 'Descendants or children. In promise passages it may have covenant significance.',
  generation: 'Can mean people living at one time, descendants, family line, or kind/type of people depending on context.',
  house: 'Can mean a building, household, family line, dynasty, temple, or people-group depending on context.',
  household: 'A family unit including relatives, servants, and dependents in ancient settings.',
  name: 'In biblical usage, name can refer not only to a label but reputation, character, authority, or presence.',
  face: 'Can mean literal face, presence, favor, or opposition depending on phrase and context.',
  hand: 'Often means power, control, agency, possession, or action, not only the body part.',
  heart: 'Often refers to the inner person: thought, will, desire, intention, and moral center.',
  bowels: 'Older English idiom for inner feelings or compassion, not merely anatomy.',
  reins: 'Older English for kidneys/inner parts, often used figuratively for deep thoughts or emotions.',
  loins: 'Can refer to the waist/hips, reproductive power, readiness for action, or descendants depending on context.',
  gird: 'Older English meaning to wrap/tie up clothing for movement or work. "Gird up" often means prepare for action.',
  girdle: 'A belt or sash used to secure garments, not modern underclothing.',
  loinsgirded: 'Idiom for readiness or preparation, because long garments were tucked up for work/travel.',
  sandals: 'Footwear. Removing sandals can signal holy ground, mourning, humility, or legal custom depending on context.',
  staff: 'A walking stick, shepherd tool, symbol of support, or authority depending on context.',
  rod: 'Can mean staff, discipline tool, measuring rod, or symbol of authority/power.',
  scepter: 'A ruler\'s staff symbolizing royal authority.',
  gate: 'City gates were places of entry, judgment, commerce, and public decision, not merely doors.',
  eldersgate: 'Elders sitting at the gate often indicates civic/legal leadership in ancient towns.',
  citygate: 'The city gate functioned as a public square, court, marketplace, and meeting place.',
  wall: 'City walls meant protection and identity. Broken walls often symbolize vulnerability or shame.',
  tower: 'A defensive structure or lookout. Figuratively it can symbolize protection or pride depending on context.',
  watchman: 'A lookout responsible to warn of danger; prophets use watchman imagery for warning and accountability.',
  shepherd: 'One who cares for sheep. Figuratively used for kings, leaders, God, or Jesus as caretaker/ruler.',
  flock: 'Can mean literal sheep/goats or figuratively God\'s people/community.',
  pasture: 'Grazing land; figuratively can suggest provision, rest, or care.',
  hireling: 'A hired worker; in John 10, contrasted with a shepherd who truly cares for the sheep.',
  vine: 'A grapevine. Figuratively can refer to Israel, fruitfulness, or in John 15 Jesus as the true vine.',
  vineyard: 'A cultivated grape field. Often used as imagery for Israel, stewardship, fruitfulness, or judgment.',
  figtree: 'A fruit tree. In biblical imagery it can signal fruitfulness, peace, judgment, or Israel depending on context.',
  olive: 'Olive tree/fruit/oil. Associated with food, light, anointing oil, and blessing.',
  anoint: 'To apply oil. It can mark consecration, healing, hospitality, or appointment to office.',
  anointed: 'One set apart or appointed, often king/priest/prophet. Messiah/Christ means Anointed One.',
  oil: 'Used for cooking, lamps, healing, hospitality, and anointing. Context decides the meaning.',
  winebibber: 'Older English insult meaning someone given to drinking wine; appears in accusations against Jesus.',
  bridegroom: 'A man getting married. Jesus uses bridegroom imagery for His presence and later expectation.',
  bride: 'A woman getting married. In biblical imagery it can represent God\'s people or the church depending on context.',
  weddingfeast: 'Wedding banquet imagery often signals joy, covenant celebration, or kingdom invitation.',
  banqueting: 'Feasting or dining. In biblical settings meals can express fellowship, honor, celebration, or conflict.',
  reclining: 'In NT meal settings people often reclined at table; this signals dining posture, not laziness.',
  bosom: 'Can mean chest/lap/close place. "Abraham\'s bosom" is an image of comfort/honor after death.',
  abrahamsbosom: 'Phrase in Luke 16 for a place of comfort/honor with Abraham; details are debated.',
  paradise: 'A place/state of blessedness. In Luke 23 and Revelation it is associated with life with God.',
  firmament: 'Older English for the expanse/sky in Genesis 1. Modern translations often use expanse.',
  deep: 'Can mean ocean depths, primeval waters, or a place/depth imagery depending on context.',
  abyss: 'Deep or bottomless place; in NT/Revelation can refer to a place associated with demonic confinement.',
  tartarus: 'Term in 2 Peter 2:4 for a place of confinement/judgment for angels; rare NT term.',
  cherub: 'Singular of cherubim, a heavenly being associated with sacred space and God\'s presence.',
  seraph: 'Singular of seraphim, heavenly being in Isaiah 6.',
  nephilim: 'Mysterious figures in Genesis 6 and Numbers 13. Interpretations vary; avoid overconfidence.',
  rephaim: 'Term connected with ancient giants/dead/shadowy figures depending on context. Interpretations vary.',
  anakim: 'Ancient people associated with great size in OT narratives.',
  amorite: 'One of the peoples in/around Canaan; sometimes used broadly for inhabitants of the land.',
  hittite: 'Ancient people/group. In OT context often listed among peoples of Canaan or surrounding regions.',
  jebusite: 'People associated with Jerusalem before David captured the city.',
  perizzite: 'One of the people groups listed in Canaan; details are limited.',
  hivite: 'One of the people groups listed in Canaan.',
  girgashite: 'One of the people groups listed in Canaan.',
  amalekite: 'People group often hostile to Israel in OT narratives.',
  edomite: 'Descendants/people of Edom, connected with Esau in biblical tradition.',
  moabite: 'Person from Moab, a nation east of the Dead Sea connected with Lot in Genesis tradition.',
  ammonite: 'Person from Ammon, a nation east of the Jordan connected with Lot in Genesis tradition.',
  philistine: 'People group on the coastal plain, often in conflict with Israel in Samuel/Kings.',
  aramean: 'Person from Aram/Syria region. Aramaic language becomes important later.',
  assyrian: 'Person/empire of Assyria, major power that conquered the northern kingdom of Israel.',
  chaldean: 'Can refer to Babylonians or a learned/astrological class depending on context.',
  magi: 'Wise men/astrologer-priests from the East in Matthew 2; not called kings in the text.',
  herodian: 'Supporter/associate of the Herodian dynasty; in the Gospels they appear in political-religious conflict.',
  zealot: 'Can mean zealous person or member/associate of a Jewish resistance movement, depending on context.',
  nicolaitans: 'Group mentioned in Revelation; exact identity and teaching are debated.',
  judaizer: 'Later term often used for those insisting Gentiles must adopt Jewish law/circumcision; not a direct biblical label.',
  gnostic: 'Later label for movements claiming special spiritual knowledge; full Gnosticism developed after the NT, so use carefully.',
  antinomian: 'Later theological label for rejecting moral obligation; not a direct biblical word.',
  legalism: 'A later label often used for misusing law/works. Be careful: biblical law observance and legalism are not always the same thing.',
  liberty: 'Freedom. In NT contexts it may refer to freedom from sin, law as covenant system, condemnation, or human masters; context decides.',
  conscience: 'Inner moral awareness or judgment. NT passages discuss weak/strong conscience and sensitivity before God.',
  stumblingblock: 'Something that causes offense, sin, or falling. Context decides whether it is a moral trap, offense, or obstacle.',
  offense: 'Can mean sin, stumbling, scandal, or cause of rejection depending on context.',
  stumbling: 'Falling or being tripped up, often figurative for sin, offense, or rejection.',
  edify: 'Older English meaning to build up, strengthen, or help another person grow.',
  edification: 'Building up or strengthening others, especially spiritually.',
  exhortation: 'Encouragement, appeal, or urgent instruction. It can be comforting or challenging.',
  longanimity: 'Older English for patience or longsuffering.',
  longsuffering: 'Patient endurance with difficult people or circumstances.',
  meekness: 'Gentleness or controlled strength, not weakness.',
  temperance: 'Self-control, especially control over desires or appetites.',
  charity: 'In older English Bible translations, often means love, not merely giving money.',
  conversation: 'In older English Bible translations, often means conduct or way of life, not only speech.',
  quicken: 'Older English meaning to make alive.',
  quickened: 'Made alive; older English form used in some translations.',
  begotten: 'Fathered/generated. In theological or familial contexts, read carefully by passage.',
  onlybegotten: 'Traditional phrase often translating unique/only one. Interpretive details vary by translation and context.',
  propitiation: 'A sacrifice or means connected with dealing with wrath/atonement. Interpretive details vary, so read with the immediate passage.',
  expiation: 'Removal or cleansing of sin/guilt. Often discussed alongside atonement language.',
  impute: 'To count, credit, or reckon something to someone.',
  reckoned: 'Counted or credited. Important in passages about righteousness, accounting, or evaluation.',
  sanctify: 'To set apart or make holy. Context decides whether status, process, or dedication is in view.',
  sanctified: 'Set apart or made holy. Context decides whether the focus is position, practice, or dedication.',
  glorify: 'To honor, make known as glorious, or bring to glory. Context decides the nuance.',
  glorified: 'Honored or brought to glory; in NT hope, can refer to future transformed life.',
  elect: 'Chosen. Context decides whether the focus is Israel, Christ, believers, a servant, or selection for a role.',
  predestination: 'Being marked out beforehand. Context and theological systems discuss details differently.',
  foreknowledge: 'Prior knowledge or relational knowing beforehand. Interpretive details vary by passage.',
  dispensation: 'Can mean stewardship/administration/arrangement in older translations, not necessarily a full theological system.',
  mystery: 'A divine truth once hidden and now revealed; not a puzzle solved by cleverness.',
  regeneration: 'Renewal or new birth language. In Matthew 19 it can refer to the renewal of all things.',
  adoption: 'Receiving the status/privilege of a son/heir. In Paul, it is family/legal imagery for belonging to God.',
  intercession: 'Appeal or prayer on behalf of another.',
  mediator: 'One who stands between parties to bring agreement or represent one to another.',
  advocate: 'Helper or representative who speaks on behalf of another.',
  paraclete: 'Helper/Advocate/Comforter; title used in John for the Holy Spirit, and related language for Jesus as advocate.',
  comforter: 'Helper, advocate, or encourager. In John, Comforter often refers to the Holy Spirit.',
  testament: 'Can mean covenant or will depending on context and translation tradition.',
  epistle: 'A letter, especially NT letters written to churches or individuals.',
  apocalypse: 'Unveiling or revelation; not simply disaster. Revelation is apocalyptic prophecy with symbolic imagery.',
  psalm: 'A sacred song, poem, or prayer, often from Israel\'s worship collection.',
  proverb: 'A compact wisdom saying, usually general wisdom rather than a mechanical promise.',
  lamentation: 'Expression of grief or mourning; Lamentations is a book of poetic grief over Jerusalem.',
  oraclebreastpiece: 'Priestly breastpiece associated with judgment/decision-making before the Lord.',
  citiesofrefuge: 'Cities where someone accused of manslaughter could flee for legal protection until trial.',
  avengerofblood: 'Family representative responsible for pursuing justice in homicide cases within OT legal structures.'
});
